  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\pi )+{f}^{\u2032}(\\pi )(x-\\pi )+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\pi )}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )=-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(\\pi )}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
